<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Future Careers – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Careers</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> To Become a Doctor, First I Will Need To...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Get the gist of dialogues in social settings. Interactive: Participate in group problem-solving sessions.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can get the gist of dialogues about career ideas by identifying main goals (e.g., "What does Mariana want to be?" "A teacher."). Can participate in group discussions on future goals, showing comprehension by contributing ideas (e.g., "What can help you become a doctor?" "Studying hard."). Can answer questions in problem-solving discussions about careers (e.g., "What does Liam plan to do next?" "He will study engineering.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards or images of various professionals (e.g., doctor, lawyer, teacher, engineer, chef). The teacher asks, "What do these people do?" and "What job do you know?" Students respond with words they know. The teacher then introduces the core vocabulary for the lesson: <i>doctor, lawyer, teacher, career, goals, internship, qualifications, plan, practice, train, achieve, succeed, prepare</i>. The teacher writes the new words on the board.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of the new vocabulary words and the target grammar structures. For example, pointing to the 'doctor' picture, the teacher says, "This is a doctor. I would like to be a doctor. I will need to study a lot." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of modals <i>should, would, will</i>, focusing on the /ʊ/ and /ɪ/ sounds. The teacher also models minimal pairs for phonemic awareness, such as /ʃ/ in "ship" and /tʃ/ in "chip," and /dʒ/ in "joke" and /tʃ/ in "choke," connecting them to relevant words if possible (e.g., 'achieve' vs. 'ship').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new vocabulary and concepts.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'career' a job for one day or for many years?" (Expected answer: Many years.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you have 'goals,' are you thinking about the past or the future?" (Expected answer: The future.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you 'train' for something, are you preparing or finishing?" (Expected answer: Preparing.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input:</b> The teacher plays an audio recording of a short dialogue (2-3 speakers) about future career plans. The dialogue includes expressions like "I want to be a doctor," "I would like to be a teacher," "What do you want to be?", "I will need to study hard," and "I plan to get good qualifications." The dialogue features the target vocabulary (<i>doctor, teacher, career, goals, plan, prepare</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, informal audio dialogue between two or three teenagers discussing their future aspirations and the steps they plan to take. (Genre: dialogue, Register: informal, Format: audio recording).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Can get the gist of simple dialogues and identify key information about future plans and career goals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (First listening - Gist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are the speakers mainly talking about?" (A. Their weekend plans / B. Their favorite food / C. Their future careers)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do they sound excited or bored about their future?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Second listening - Specific information):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen again and answer True/False questions (e.g., "Mariana wants to be a lawyer." "Liam plans to study engineering after school.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match each speaker to their main career goal.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus:</b> Listening for specific details, distinguishing pronunciation of modals and key vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using Wh-questions for career goals ("What do you want to be?"), future tense for plans ("I will need to..."), modals for intention and suggestions ("I would like to...", "You should..."), and "want to + infinitive verb" to express intentions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen and Complete):</b> The teacher distributes a transcript of the dialogue with key words (target vocabulary and grammar structures like 'want to be,' 'will need to,' 'would like to') blanked out. Students listen to the audio again (third time) and fill in the missing words. Teacher checks answers as a class, focusing on spelling and meaning.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion & Suggestion):</b> The teacher provides sentence starters related to career goals, and students listen and complete them based on new input. Example: Teacher says, "I want to be a..." and shows a picture of a lawyer. Students listen and complete orally or in writing "I want to be a <b>lawyer</b>." Then, the teacher prompts, "To be a lawyer, you will need to..." and students listen to suggestions and choose an appropriate one (e.g., "You will need to <b>study law</b>" or "You will need to get good <b>qualifications</b>"). The teacher emphasizes clear pronunciation of modals and new vocabulary.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen and Clarify):</b> Students work in pairs. Partner A makes a statement about a career goal using "I want to be a..." or "I would like to be a..." (e.g., "I want to be a doctor."). Partner B listens carefully and asks a follow-up Wh-question (e.g., "What will you need to do to achieve that goal?"). Partner A responds using "I will need to..." or "I plan to...". Students swap roles. This activity focuses on active listening and using the target grammar to ask and answer questions about future plans.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: Group Career Planning Discussion</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each student takes turns stating their future career goal (e.g., "I want to be a teacher because I like helping others." or "I would like to be a professional lawyer."). After each student shares their goal, the other members of the group listen and provide suggestions or ask questions about what the speaker will need to do to achieve their goal (e.g., "You will need to get good qualifications." "What specific subjects will you need to study?"). The focus is on active listening, understanding others' intentions, and contributing constructive ideas using target grammar (Wh-questions, future tense, modals) and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative group discussion/problem-solving session.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> Directly addresses "To Become a Doctor, First I Will Need To..." and "Future Careers" by having students articulate their aspirations and consider the steps needed to achieve them. The activity is designed for students to practice listening to others' plans and offering relevant suggestions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the group discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Group Career Planning Discussion." The teacher observes each student's ability to listen to others' career goals, ask relevant Wh-questions, and contribute appropriate suggestions or future plans using the target language. The teacher may also ask individuals follow-up questions about their group members' plans (e.g., "What does Liam want to be?" "What will Mariana need to do to achieve her goal?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of target vocabulary and modals (should, would, will).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Incorporates words like <i>doctor, lawyer, teacher, career, goals, plan, qualifications</i>.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> Correctly uses Wh-questions for goals, "I will need to...", "I would like to...", "I want to + infinitive."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and relevance:</b> Contributions are clear and relevant to the discussion about career goals and plans.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Active listening and collaboration:</b> Shows comprehension by responding appropriately and engaging in problem-solving discussions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List three new words about careers or plans you learned today."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one thing you learned from listening to your classmates' career plans?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me today when listening to discussions about future careers?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging for me when trying to understand others' plans or when making suggestions?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully get the gist of the dialogues about career ideas? Were they able to identify main goals?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students actively participate in group problem-solving, showing comprehension by contributing ideas and answering questions?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What vocabulary or grammar points need further reinforcement? Were there any common pronunciation difficulties with modals?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed to support students' listening comprehension and interactive skills in future lessons?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), we will build on the vocabulary and concepts introduced today. We will read short descriptive texts about individuals pursuing these careers and the steps they are taking, which will reinforce our understanding of career paths and the associated vocabulary."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>